      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Power electronics and inverters\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Inverters are essential power electronics devices that convert direct current (DC) to alternating current (AC), enabling renewable energy sources like solar panels and batteries to integrate with electrical grids that operate on AC power\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Three main types of inverters exist: string inverters, microinverters, and central inverters, each with different applications in residential, commercial, and utility-scale renewable energy systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Power electronics efficiency in inverters typically ranges from 94-99% for modern devices, with losses occurring primarily through switching operations and heat dissipation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ieee.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Silicon (Si) and silicon carbide (SiC) are the primary semiconductor materials used in modern power electronics, with SiC offering higher efficiency and temperature tolerance for advanced applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Grid-tied inverters must include voltage and frequency regulation capabilities to synchronize renewable energy generation with existing AC grid standards and maintain grid stability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Power factor correction in inverters helps optimize energy transfer and reduces reactive power requirements in electrical systems connected to renewable sources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Multilevel inverter topologies (neutral point clamped, flying capacitor, and modular multilevel) are increasingly used in high-voltage and high-power renewable applications to improve output waveform quality\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ieee.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wide-bandgap semiconductors like gallium nitride (GaN) are emerging alternatives to traditional silicon with potential for higher switching frequencies and improved thermal performance\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
