      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydrogen fuel and fuel cells\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen fuel cells generate electricity through an electrochemical reaction between hydrogen and oxygen, producing only water as a byproduct, making them zero-emission when powered by green hydrogen\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Green hydrogen is produced by electrolysis using renewable electricity, while gray hydrogen accounts for approximately 95% of current global hydrogen production and is derived from natural gas reforming\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Proton exchange membrane (PEM) fuel cells are the most developed type for vehicle applications and can achieve electrical efficiency of 40-60%\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fuelcellsworks.com\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Major automakers including Toyota (Mirai), Hyundai (Nexo), and Honda have commercialized hydrogen fuel cell vehicles, with approximately 15,000-20,000 fuel cell vehicles in operation globally as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen fuel cells are used for stationary power generation, backup power systems, and industrial applications including ammonia and methanol production\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The global hydrogen market was valued at approximately $136 billion in 2022 and is projected to grow significantly with increasing green hydrogen capacity investments\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen storage and distribution infrastructure remains a major challenge, with costs of hydrogen production, storage, and transportation currently higher than fossil fuels in most regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The U.S. Department of Energy has set a target to reduce green hydrogen production costs to $1 per kilogram by 2030 through the Hydrogen and Fuel Cell Hubs program\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
