      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Carbon capture and renewable integration\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct Air Capture (DAC) technology can be powered by renewable energy sources like wind and solar to create a carbon-negative system when combined with permanent storage or utilization methods\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/direct-air-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that carbon capture, utilization and storage (CCUS) could account for up to 14% of cumulative CO2 reductions needed by 2070 to meet net-zero targets\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Point-source carbon capture from power plants and industrial facilities can be integrated with renewable energy systems to reduce overall facility emissions while maintaining productive output\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/fecm/carbon-capture-utilization-and-storage-ccus\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable energy integration with carbon capture requires significant electrical power; a single large DAC facility can consume 0.5-1 MWh of electricity per ton of CO2 captured\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.carbonbrief.org/direct-air-capture-is-it-ready-to-play-a-major-role-in-tackling-climate-change\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of direct air capture with renewable energy is estimated between $200-600 USD per ton of CO2, with costs declining as renewable energy becomes cheaper\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-023-00023-2\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Australia, Canada, and Iceland are leading in deployment of DAC facilities powered by renewable energy sources, particularly hydroelectric and geothermal power\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.carbontracker.org/reports/direct-air-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Some renewable energy facilities now incorporate on-site carbon capture systems to offset operational emissions or create revenue through captured CO2 utilization in beverages, chemicals, and construction materials\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/sustainability/our-insights/carbon-capture-utilization-and-storage-a-critical-lever-for-net-zero\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
