      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Ocean thermal energy conversion (OTEC)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC is a renewable energy technology that exploits the temperature difference between warm surface seawater and cold deep seawater to generate electricity, with typical temperature differences of 20\u00b0C or more required for efficient operation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy10osti/48575.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The theoretical global OTEC potential is estimated at approximately 3-5 terawatts of power in tropical and subtropical ocean regions between 20\u00b0N and 20\u00b0S latitude\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC systems operate on the Rankine thermodynamic cycle, using a working fluid with a low boiling point that circulates through evaporators and condensers to drive a turbine\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power-technologies-office/about-ocean-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The first experimental OTEC plant was built in Hawaii in 1979 and produced 15 kilowatts of electricity, demonstrating the feasibility of the technology\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ee.hawaii.edu/~jdrees/otec_overview.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"As of 2024, there are no large-scale commercial OTEC plants currently operating, though several pilot and demonstration projects are under development globally\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/Energy-Transition/Technology-Innovation/Ocean-Energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC systems can also produce fresh water as a byproduct through desalination processes, and potentially support mariculture and other marine applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy10osti/48575.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The main technical challenges for OTEC deployment include biofouling of heat exchangers, high capital costs, and the need for specialized infrastructure in deep ocean environments\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0360319921000288\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC is classified as a baseload renewable energy source because ocean temperature gradients remain relatively stable year-round, providing consistent power generation capacity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power-technologies-office/about-ocean-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
