      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Waste-to-energy systems\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Waste-to-energy (WtE) systems convert municipal solid waste into electricity and heat through combustion, gasification, or anaerobic digestion processes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.epa.gov/waste/energy-recovery-waste\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern waste-to-energy facilities can recover approximately 600-800 kWh of electricity per ton of waste processed\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.wte.org/about-wte/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Europe leads global waste-to-energy adoption with over 490 WtE plants operating, primarily in Germany, France, and Scandinavia as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.epa.gov/waste/energy-recovery-waste\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Anaerobic digestion of organic waste produces biogas containing 50-70% methane, which can be used for electricity generation or heating\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy13osti/57157.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Waste-to-energy systems can divert 50-90% of waste from landfills depending on waste composition and facility design\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iswa.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Advanced gasification and pyrolysis technologies for waste-to-energy are being developed to improve efficiency and reduce emissions compared to conventional combustion\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021/Mar/Renewable-energy-integration\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The United States generates approximately 4.5 million tons of municipal solid waste annually, with about 12-13% processed through waste-to-energy facilities\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.epa.gov/facts-and-figures-about-materials-waste-and-recycling\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Waste-to-energy facilities must meet strict air quality regulations including limits on dioxins, mercury, and particulate matter emissions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.epa.gov/air-emissions-inventories/ap-42-compilation-air-pollutant-emission-factors\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Landfill gas capture systems can extract methane from existing landfills to generate electricity, representing a form of distributed waste-to-energy\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.epa.gov/lmop/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The global waste-to-energy market is projected to grow at a CAGR of 5-7% through 2030, driven by increasing waste generation and energy demands\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.grandviewresearch.com/industry-analysis/waste-to-energy-market\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
