      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Tidal and wave energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy is generated from the gravitational pull of the moon and sun on Earth's oceans, creating predictable and consistent tidal cycles that occur twice daily in most locations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/tidal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy is generated by wind blowing across ocean surfaces, transferring energy to water and creating waves that can be captured through various technologies\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal stream generators work similarly to wind turbines but operate underwater, with rotors that spin from tidal currents to generate electricity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/tidal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal barrages operate as dams across tidal estuaries, capturing water at high tide and releasing it through turbines at low tide\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy converters include point absorbers, attenuators, and terminators that capture energy from wave motion through various mechanical and hydraulic systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy resources are highly location-dependent, with the most viable sites having tidal ranges exceeding 5 meters or tidal current speeds exceeding 1 meter per second\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The energy density of tidal and wave resources is significantly higher than wind or solar, with ocean water being approximately 800 times denser than air\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/tidal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy potential is estimated at approximately 2-3 kW per meter of wave front in favorable ocean locations globally\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns for tidal and wave energy include impacts on marine ecosystems, fish migration patterns, and potential electromagnetic effects from underwater cables\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commercial tidal energy deployment is most advanced in the United Kingdom, Canada, and South Korea, with several operational utility-scale installations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy technology remains predominantly in demonstration and pilot phases globally, with limited commercial deployment compared to tidal energy\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Capital costs for tidal energy installations are estimated at $4-6 million per megawatt, with wave energy costs currently higher due to less mature technology\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
