      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Algae and advanced biofuels\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Algae can produce biofuels through multiple pathways including biodiesel, bioethanol, biogas, and biohydrogen, with some algae species capable of producing oil content up to 50% of their dry biomass weight\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/bioenergy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Algae-based biofuels have a significantly smaller carbon footprint than terrestrial crops because algae absorbs CO2 during growth and doesn't require arable land or freshwater irrigation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy09/45503.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The U.S. Department of Energy's Algae Raceway Pond program aims to reduce algae biofuel production costs to $5 per gallon by improving cultivation and processing technologies\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/bioenergy/algae-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microalgae species like Chlorella and Scenedesmus are among the most studied for biofuel production due to their high growth rates and lipid content\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmars.2020.00084/full\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current challenges in algae biofuel commercialization include high harvesting and dewatering costs, which can represent 20-30% of total production expenses\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/bioenergy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wastewater and flue gas from industrial sources can be used as nutrient sources for algae cultivation, creating a circular economy approach to biofuel production\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0360319915300495\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Genetically modified algae strains are being developed to increase lipid production rates and improve biofuel yields compared to wild-type strains\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-26235-6\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Algae cultivation systems can be open-pond based or closed photobioreactors, with closed systems offering better control but higher capital costs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/bioenergy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
