      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydroelectric and hydrokinetic power\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydropower is the world's largest source of renewable electricity, accounting for approximately 16% of global electricity generation as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydropower-and-its-contribution-to-decarbonisation\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Conventional hydroelectric power involves building dams to create reservoirs that store water at height, which is then released through turbines to generate electricity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-hydroelectric-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrokinetic energy systems, also called in-stream hydrokinetic devices, generate electricity from flowing water without requiring large dams or reservoirs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power/hydrokinetic-and-wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Run-of-river hydroelectric systems operate by diverting a portion of river flow through turbines without creating large storage reservoirs, minimizing environmental impact compared to traditional dams\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2022-May/renewable-capacity-statistics-2022\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Pumped-storage hydroelectricity acts as an energy storage system by pumping water uphill during off-peak hours and releasing it through turbines during peak demand\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.hydropower.org/technology/storage-and-pumped-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrokinetic devices such as tidal stream generators and wave energy converters are still largely in development and demonstration phases, with limited commercial deployment as of 2024\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/water/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydropower facilities have a typical lifespan of 50-100 years and can operate with capacity factors of 40-50% or higher, making them among the most reliable renewable energy sources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydropower-can-support-energy-security-and-grid-stability\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns with large hydroelectric dams include altered river ecosystems, fish migration disruption, and methane emissions from reservoir decomposition\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.worldwildlife.org/threats/dams-threaten-rivers-and-species\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy, a form of hydrokinetic power, has predictable generation patterns because tidal cycles follow lunar cycles, offering advantages over solar and wind for grid planning\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/tidal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"As of 2023, global hydropower capacity exceeded 1,400 GW, with China, Canada, Brazil, and the United States being the leading hydropower producers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.hydropower.org/resource-centre/hydropower-status-report\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
