  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Wind energy (onshore and offshore)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy is the fastest-growing renewable energy source globally, with global wind power capacity reaching over 1,000 GW as of 2022\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind turbines typically have capacity factors of 25-35%, while offshore wind turbines achieve capacity factors of 40-50% due to stronger and more consistent winds\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern onshore wind turbines have rotor diameters of 100-150 meters and hub heights of 80-120 meters, while offshore turbines are increasingly 12-15 MW with larger dimensions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.windeurope.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind has higher capital costs ($3-5 million per MW) compared to onshore wind ($1-2 million per MW), but benefits from higher energy yields and less land-use conflict\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Jun/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy produces electricity with minimal greenhouse gas emissions over its lifecycle (10-20 grams CO2 equivalent per kilowatt-hour), making it one of the cleanest energy sources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind farms require 360 square kilometers per GW of installed capacity when accounting for spacing requirements, while offshore wind requires significantly less surface area\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind turbine manufacturing, transportation, and installation are primary cost drivers for both onshore and offshore projects, representing 40-60% of total capital expenditure\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind development requires specialized installation vessels, submarine cables, and marine infrastructure, with installation costs representing a larger proportion of total project costs than onshore\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.windeurope.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy variability and intermittency require grid integration solutions including energy storage, smart grids, and geographic diversification across multiple wind farms\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental considerations for onshore wind include bird and bat mortality, while offshore wind impacts include marine ecosystem effects, noise, and electromagnetic field effects from submarine cables\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.birdlife.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"China leads global wind energy production with approximately 370 GW of installed capacity, followed by the United States and Germany\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating offshore wind technology enables deployment in deeper waters (>60 meters), expanding potential installation sites beyond fixed-foundation limitations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
