  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hybrid renewable systems\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid renewable systems combine two or more renewable energy sources (such as solar, wind, hydro, and biomass) with energy storage and smart controls to provide continuous, reliable power generation and improve overall system efficiency and reliability.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2020/Apr/Renewable-capacity-statistics-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Solar-wind hybrid systems are among the most commonly deployed hybrid configurations globally, as solar and wind resources often have complementary generation patterns (solar peaks during day, wind can peak at night or during cloudy periods).\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/73262.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Battery energy storage systems (BESS) are critical components in hybrid renewable systems, addressing intermittency challenges and enabling load-following capabilities and grid stabilization.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid renewable systems with integrated storage can achieve capacity factors of 50-80%, compared to 25-35% for standalone solar or wind systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2019/Feb/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2019\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid systems reduce the total installed capacity needed compared to standalone systems while maintaining equivalent power output, leading to reduced land use requirements and environmental impact.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy17osti/68695.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microgrids utilizing hybrid renewable systems can operate in islanded mode (independent from main grid) during grid disturbances or emergencies, improving energy resilience for remote and rural areas.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/oe/articles/microgrids-and-resilience\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) for hybrid renewable systems has declined 40-50% over the past decade due to decreasing component costs (especially solar PV and batteries) and improved system integration technologies.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2020/Jun/Renewable-Cost-Database\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid systems incorporating pumped hydro storage with solar and wind can provide multi-hour to seasonal energy storage, enabling long-duration energy shifting and grid balancing.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2020/Sep/Electricity-storage-and-renewables\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Advanced control systems and artificial intelligence are increasingly used in hybrid renewable systems to optimize energy dispatch, predict generation patterns, and manage frequency and voltage stability.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/smart-grids\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Diesel-renewable hybrids (combining solar/wind with diesel generators) are widely deployed in off-grid and remote locations, reducing fuel consumption by 40-60% compared to diesel-only systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy18osti/70723.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
