      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems involve artificially creating or improving permeability in hot rock formations by hydraulic stimulation to enable economical heat extraction in areas without natural geothermal reservoirs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/74697.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS technology requires creating fracture networks in crystalline basement rock at depths typically between 3-5 kilometers to circulate fluid and extract heat\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/22558191\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The Soultz-sous-For\u00eats project in France is the world's first EGS demonstration plant that successfully generated electricity commercially from an EGS reservoir\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.geothermal-energy.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS can potentially provide baseload power generation with capacity factors of 70-90%, making it comparable to conventional power plants\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.pnas.org/content/117/6/2797\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Technical challenges for EGS include induced seismicity from hydraulic stimulation, managing fluid loss, and achieving economic viability at commercial scale\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/geothermal\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS development costs remain higher than conventional geothermal, with levelized costs of electricity estimated between $100-300/MWh depending on site-specific conditions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1773850\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Unlike conventional geothermal systems, EGS can theoretically be deployed globally since heat exists everywhere at sufficient depths, not limited to tectonically active regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-enhanced-geothermal-systems-egs\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Recent EGS projects in Australia, South Korea, and the United States have demonstrated technical progress and attracting increased venture capital investment\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/geothermal\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS requires significant water usage for hydraulic stimulation and continuous circulation, raising environmental sustainability concerns in water-scarce regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306261917312066\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The U.S. Department of Energy's FORGE project aims to demonstrate economically viable EGS by 2030 through site development and technology testing\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
