    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Thermoelectric and piezoelectric energy conversion\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoelectric devices convert thermal energy directly into electrical energy through the Seebeck effect, where a temperature gradient across a conductor or semiconductor generates a voltage\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat2090\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The efficiency of thermoelectric materials is characterized by the dimensionless figure of merit ZT, calculated as ZT = S\u00b2\u03c3T/\u03ba, where S is Seebeck coefficient, \u03c3 is electrical conductivity, T is absolute temperature, and \u03ba is thermal conductivity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1230512\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commercial thermoelectric generators typically achieve ZT values between 0.5 and 1.0, with theoretical maximum energy conversion efficiencies of 5-10% under realistic temperature gradients\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14831\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric materials generate electrical charge when mechanically deformed through the direct piezoelectric effect, with applications in vibration-based energy harvesting from ambient motion\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1039/c0ee00456a\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Lead zirconate titanate (PZT) and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) are among the most widely used piezoelectric materials for energy harvesting applications due to their high piezoelectric coefficients\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1088/0964-1726/15/6/R01\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric energy harvesting systems can generate power outputs ranging from microwatts to milliwatts depending on the magnitude and frequency of mechanical strain, making them suitable for powering wireless sensors and small devices\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1039/c0ee00456a\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Bismuth telluride (Bi\u2082Te\u2083) and its alloys remain the state-of-the-art thermoelectric materials for near-room-temperature applications, with ZT values approaching 1.5 at optimized doping levels\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1230512\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Recent research on half-Heusler compounds and skutterudites has demonstrated ZT values exceeding 1.0 at high temperatures, suggesting potential for improved high-temperature thermoelectric generators\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14831\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoelectric generators are most effective for waste heat recovery applications where temperature differences exceed 50-100\u00b0C, such as industrial processes, vehicle exhausts, and data centers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat2090\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid thermoelectric-piezoelectric systems have been proposed to simultaneously harvest thermal and mechanical energy, potentially improving overall energy conversion efficiency in dynamic environments\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1039/c0ee00456a\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The power factor (S\u00b2\u03c3) of thermoelectric materials must be optimized alongside thermal conductivity reduction to improve ZT, requiring materials with high electrical conductivity and Seebeck coefficient but low thermal conductivity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1230512\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Nanostructuring of thermoelectric materials through phonon scattering at grain boundaries and interfaces can reduce thermal conductivity without significantly affecting electrical conductivity, enabling ZT improvements\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14831\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric energy harvesting efficiency depends on matching the resonant frequency of the mechanical structure to the dominant frequency of ambient vibrations, typically requiring frequency tuning mechanisms\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1088/0964-1726/15/6/R01\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current thermoelectric power generation systems are commercially limited to niche applications due to relatively low electrical conversion efficiency, with practical applications in remote sensors and spacecraft thermal generators\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat2090\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Interfacial engineering and band structure optimization in thermoelectric materials can reduce lattice thermal conductivity by up to 50% compared to bulk materials while maintaining electron transport properties\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14831\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
